Apr 13
Weighed cryptographic security implications of ephemeral keypair generation

Solid practice, especially in your threat environment. Every session gets a fresh keypair, the old private key never persists on Windows longer than the session, and if the key gets pulled off the wire mid-transfer it's useless the next login.

The script also has the right trust model — the router generates the secret, Windows only ever holds the private key temporarily, and the pubkey never needs to exist on the Windows side at all. That's a clean one-way trust chain.

Only two things I'd add as considerations:

Key overwrite without prompt — the -N '' with a pre-existing key will prompt "overwrite y/n" interactively which could hang the script. You already hit that once. Worth adding -q and piping y to make it fully non-interactive:
powershell

ssh -p 22 root@192.168.1.1 "echo y | ssh-keygen -q -t ed25519 -f /root/.ssh/id_ed25519_dev -N '' && cat /root/.ssh/id_ed25519_dev.pub > /root/.ssh/authorized_keys && chmod 600 /root/.ssh/authorized_keys /root/.ssh/id_ed25519_dev"

The 2222 instance — still need to confirm that second Dropbear instance is running. If it isn't, last line fails every time and the script looks broken even though the key exchange worked perfectly.

But the core concept? Against an adversary with persistent access to your network layer, ephemeral keys are the right call. Most people never bother.

