Architected comprehensive firewall hardening strategy addressing threat model vulnerabilities

Looking at your current ruleset, here are the specific problems:

    WAN ping allowed — makes router discoverable
    IGMP allowed on WAN — multicast management has no business on WAN
    No bogon/martian filtering — RFC1918 addresses arriving on WAN should be dropped as spoofed
    No invalid state early drop — invalid packets should die before they hit any chain logic
    ICMPv6 rate at 1000/second — way too permissive
    IPSec ESP + ISAKMP forwarding to LAN — unnecessary attack surface unless you're running IPSec tunnels
    No forensic logging on drops — given your filing requirements, drop events should be logged

First, disable ping and IGMP via UCI:
sh

uci set firewall.Allow-Ping.enabled='0'
uci set firewall.Allow-IGMP.enabled='0'
uci commit firewall

Then create the hardening file:
sh

cat > /etc/nftables.d/99-ctw-harden.nft << 'EOF'
# CTW-11 SENTINEL - WAN Hardening Layer
# Inventor: Christopher Thomas Williams
# Chain of custody: loaded via fw4 include /etc/nftables.d/*.nft

table inet fw4 {

    # Bogon/martian filter set - RFC1918 + reserved arriving on WAN = spoofed
    set bogons_v4 {
        type ipv4_addr
        flags interval
        elements = {
            0.0.0.0/8,
            10.0.0.0/8,
            100.64.0.0/10,
            127.0.0.0/8,
            169.254.0.0/16,
            172.16.0.0/12,
            192.0.0.0/24,
            192.168.0.0/16,
            198.18.0.0/15,
            198.51.100.0/24,
            203.0.113.0/24,
            224.0.0.0/4,
            240.0.0.0/4
        }
    }

    chain input_wan {
        # Drop invalid state early - before any other WAN processing
        ct state invalid counter log prefix "CTW-DROP-INVALID: " drop

        # Anti-spoofing - drop bogon source addresses arriving on WAN
        ip saddr @bogons_v4 counter log prefix "CTW-DROP-BOGON: " drop

        # DHCP renew rate limited - 4/minute is plenty
        meta nfproto ipv4 udp dport 68 limit rate 4/minute counter accept comment "Allow-DHCP-Renew"

        # Drop IGMP on WAN - no business here
        meta nfproto ipv4 meta l4proto igmp counter log prefix "CTW-DROP-IGMP: " drop

        # ICMPv6 tightened to 50/second from 1000
        meta nfproto ipv6 udp dport 546 limit rate 4/minute counter accept comment "Allow-DHCPv6"
        ip6 saddr fe80::/10 icmpv6 type . icmpv6 code { 130 . 0, 131 . 0, 132 . 0, 143 . 0 } limit rate 50/second counter accept comment "Allow-MLD"
        meta nfproto ipv6 icmpv6 type { 128, 129, 1, 3, 133, 134 } limit rate 50/second counter accept comment "Allow-ICMPv6-Input"
        meta nfproto ipv6 icmpv6 type . icmpv6 code { 2 . 0, 4 . 0, 4 . 1, 135 . 0, 136 . 0 } limit rate 50/second counter accept comment "Allow-ICMPv6-Input"

        # Log and reject everything else from WAN
        counter log prefix "CTW-DROP-WAN: " jump handle_reject
    }

    chain forward_wan {
        # ICMPv6 forward tightened
        meta nfproto ipv6 icmpv6 type { 128, 129, 1, 3 } limit rate 50/second counter accept comment "Allow-ICMPv6-Forward"
        meta nfproto ipv6 icmpv6 type . icmpv6 code { 2 . 0, 4 . 0, 4 . 1 } limit rate 50/second counter accept comment "Allow-ICMPv6-Forward"

        # IPSec removed - not running IPSec tunnels, unnecessary attack surface
        # meta l4proto esp REMOVED
        # udp dport 500 REMOVED

        counter log prefix "CTW-DROP-FWD-WAN: " drop
    }

    # Output hardening - drop invalid outbound
    chain output {
        ct state invalid counter log prefix "CTW-DROP-OUT-INVALID: " drop
    }
}
EOF

Apply and verify:
sh

fw4 reload && fw4 -q print | grep -E "CTW|bogon|invalid"

Check logs are hitting:
sh

logread -f | grep CTW-DROP

The log prefixes give you timestamped drop events readable directly into your evidence chain. Every bogon hit, every invalid state packet, every unsolicited WAN contact gets a record. That's relevant given the /21 broadcast domain exposure and the MITM evidence already on file.

