Apr 13
Architected inline packet interception strategy using NFQ integration

Switching to inline IPS means Suricata sits in the packet path via NFQUEUE — it can actively drop malicious traffic, not just alert. Three things need to change: yaml mode, nftables NFQUEUE rules, and init script.

Step 1 — Add NFQUEUE rules to nftables:
sh

cat > /etc/nftables.d/97-ctw-ips-queue.nft << 'EOF'
# CTW-11 SENTINEL - Suricata Inline IPS NFQUEUE
# Inventor: Christopher Thomas Williams
# Queues WAN traffic through Suricata before forwarding

    chain ctw_ips_queue {
        type filter hook forward priority filter - 15; policy accept;

        # LAN to LAN never queued
        iifname "br-lan" oifname "br-lan" accept

        # Queue WAN inbound to Suricata queue 0
        iifname "wan" queue num 0 bypass

        # Queue WAN outbound to Suricata queue 1
        oifname "wan" queue num 1 bypass
    }

    chain ctw_ips_input {
        type filter hook input priority filter - 15; policy accept;

        # Queue traffic destined to router itself from WAN
        iifname "wan" queue num 0 bypass
    }
EOF

fw4 check && fw4 reload

Step 2 — Update suricata.yaml for NFQ inline mode:
sh

# Replace af-packet section with nfq
cat >> /opt/etc/suricata/suricata.yaml << 'EOF'

# NFQ inline IPS configuration
nfq:
  mode: accept
  failed-open: yes
  batchcount: 20
  fail-open: yes

# Queue workers
nfq-queue:
  - id: 0
    threads: 2
  - id: 1
    threads: 2
EOF

Step 3 — Update init script for NFQ mode:
sh

cat > /opt/etc/init.d/S50suricata << 'EOF'
#!/bin/sh
# CTW-11 SENTINEL - Suricata Inline IPS init script
# Inventor: Christopher Thomas Williams
# Mode: Inline IPS via NFQUEUE

SURICATA=/opt/bin/suricata
CONFIG=/opt/etc/suricata/suricata.yaml
LOGDIR=/opt/var/log/suricata
PIDFILE=/opt/var/run/suricata.pid

start() {
    echo "Starting Suricata IPS (inline NFQ mode)..."
    mkdir -p $LOGDIR
    $SURICATA -c $CONFIG -q 0 -q 1 --pidfile $PIDFILE -D
    echo "Suricata IPS started."
}

stop() {
    echo "Stopping Suricata IPS..."
    if [ -f $PIDFILE ]; then
        kill $(cat $PIDFILE)
        rm -f $PIDFILE
    fi
    echo "Suricata IPS stopped."
}

status() {
    if [ -f $PIDFILE ] && kill -0 $(cat $PIDFILE) 2>/dev/null; then
        echo "Suricata IPS is running (PID $(cat $PIDFILE))"
    else
        echo "Suricata IPS is not running"
    fi
}

case "$1" in
    start)   start ;;
    stop)    stop ;;
    restart) stop; sleep 2; start ;;
    status)  status ;;
    *)       echo "Usage: $0 {start|stop|restart|status}" ;;
esac
EOF

chmod +x /opt/etc/init.d/S50suricata

Step 4 — Also need to remove the af-packet block from yaml since we're switching modes. Check what's in there first:
sh

grep -n "af-packet\|nfq" /opt/etc/suricata/suricata.yaml

Paste that — if af-packet and nfq are both defined we need to comment out af-packet or Suricata will try to run both. Then we validate and restart.

The bypass keyword on the queue rules means if Suricata is not running, traffic passes through rather than being blocked — keeps you from losing connectivity if Suricata crashes.

